From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jonathan Schwender <schwenderjonathan@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 14 Oct 2026 09:59:11 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] script: Honor preload=none, and resume the load when playback
 is requested

The resource fetch algorithm suspended the fetch for preload=none only
when the can-autoplay flag was false, which is the case once play() or
pause() ran before resource selection, and nothing ever resumed it. So
preload=none was ignored while the element was not asked to play, and an
element that was asked to play, e.g. with `src`, `load()` and `play()`,
never loaded or played.

Like Gecko and Blink, suspend the fetch for preload=none only while the
element is paused, has no autoplay attribute and is not loading through an
explicit `load()`, and never for a blob URL, which is a local resource.
Suspend before creating a media player. Resume, with steps 5.remote.1.5
to 5.remote.1.7 and after the task that stops delaying the load event,
when `play()` is called, the autoplay attribute is set, or the preload
attribute changes to another state.

As in other browsers, a `<video preload=none>` that is not playing no
longer fetches its metadata, so it has no natural size until it plays.

Fork note: meant for upstream, with the three patches before (it uses
patch 69's network-state setter). Drop once upstream has it.

Testing: Adds a WPT test that play() plays a media element with
preload=none, also with source elements; three of its six subtests time
out without this change.

Validation: Release builds of upstream f040186a7ee with the series, against the same build without this patch, a direct check of preload=none cases: `play()` right after setting `src`, after `load()`, and after `pause()` now load and play (before, `readyState` stayed 0 and the `play()` promise never settled); without a request to play, the element now stays at NETWORK_IDLE and HAVE_NOTHING instead of downloading the resource; `play()`, setting `preload` to auto and setting the autoplay attribute resume the load; `load()` alone, the autoplay attribute, blob URLs and `load()` with a first source element that is unsupported or fails still load right away; `load()` followed by a `src` change suspends (like Blink); setting `currentTime` while suspended doesn't resume. The new test: 3 of 6 subtests time out without this patch, 6 of 6 pass with it (3 runs), and chrome-headless-shell 154 passes 6 of 6. `./mach test-wpt --processes 4` of the same 461 media tests as for the previous patches: only the new test changes, apart from tests that are intermittent with both builds. Unreferenced preload=none elements with `play()` now play to the end and are collected afterwards (before, they never played and were kept alive). Two rounds of independent agent review; all should-fix findings are fixed (explicit `load()` now covers every source candidate of the resource selection, blob URLs are never suspended, a resume before the stop-delaying task waits for it, so `suspend` sees NETWORK_IDLE); not done: gating the autoplay override on the sandboxed automatic features flag, whose only effect is an extra fetch.
AI-assisted: Yes (agent session; root-caused, written and tested by the agent)
Signed-off-by: Jonathan Schwender <schwenderjonathan@gmail.com>
